"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[636],{2636:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2020-12-01T09:06:00","modified":"2020-12-01T09:06:00","id":1043,"title":"Packaging a Tree-shakeable Library (late 2020)","article":"<h2 id=\\"tldr\\">TL:DR;</h2>\\r\\n<p>How to make a library, that is fairly side effect free, so applications that install the library can tree shake it, but that also deploys to multiple different targets (environments), including directly in the browser.</p>\\r\\n<h2 id=\\"background\\">Background</h2>\\r\\n<p>I was looking at archiving several old open source libraries recently, but one caught my eye. It was a fairly simple pure  library (with no side effects) that was still useable, called fn-validate, in fact I had used it myself recently and it was open to tree-shaking. So I thought lets update it!... And that&#39;s when the tricky part started.</p>\\r\\n<h2 id=\\"goal\\">Goal</h2>\\r\\n<p>First thing I worked out what I wanted to support:</p>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li>ES Modules that can be tree-shaken by bundlers.</li>\\r\\n<li>ES Modules that can be loaded directly by browsers that support them.</li>\\r\\n<li>ES Modules that are only compiled to environments that support ES Modules directly, keeping the code lightweight and small as possible.</li>\\r\\n<li>IIFE so that older browsers (down to IE11 in my case) could load the library directly.</li>\\r\\n<li>CommonJS so that Node could used the library with <code>const mylibrary = require(&#39;mylibrary&#39;);</code>.</li>\\r\\n<li>CommonJS compiled to run on Node versions (8+) but also that would run on IE11 in case bundled.</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n<p>As of v13.2.0 Node had switched support for native ES Modules from experimental to stable and with it a new way for telling applications that install the library as a package where its code entry point is, so needed to handle that as well.</p>\\r\\n<p>So I decided to build the code to run in 2 different environments: </p>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><code>legacy</code> anything IE 11+ and Node, bundled into CJS and IIFE format</li>\\r\\n<li><code>modern</code> anything that can handle ES Modules directly, and bundled into ES Modules format only.</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n<p>The <code>modern</code> build would be smaller than the <code>legacy</code> build as less features would need to be converted to older formats, but should also be faster due to using built-in features rather than polyfills. Additionally as it is only ES Modules it could be tree-shaken by a bundler and its sized reduced further.</p>\\r\\n<h2 id=\\"the-code\\">The Code</h2>\\r\\n<h3 id=\\"side-effects\\">Side Effects</h3>\\r\\n<p>First thing to think is your library suitable for tree shaking? </p>\\r\\n<p>It should have many (not all) of its modules <a href=\\"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/a/40314\\">side effect free</a> that can be excluded if the consumer of the library doesnt use them. If the library isnt, either its not suitable for tree-shaking (which is doesnt make it bad!) or it will require some work cleaning the library up first.</p>\\r\\n<p>If it is suitable we want to give a bundler the greatest chance at tree-shaking we need to tell any applications that install this library that it is side effect free in the package.json file. </p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// package.json</span>\\r\\n{\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&quot;sideEffects&quot;</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">false</span>\\r\\n}</code></pre>\\r\\n<p>Bundlers like webpack and rollup understand they can be a lot more aggressive with code that is side effect free. </p>\\r\\n<p><em><strong>NB:</strong> If some parts of your library are not side effect free then you can let the bundler know which files arent explicitly listing them in <code>package.json</code> which gives the bundler the ability to tree-shake the rest. See <a href=\\"https://webpack.js.org/guides/tree-shaking/#mark-the-file-as-side-effect-free\\">Webpack&#39;s description for more options on side effects</a>.</em></p>\\r\\n<h3 id=\\"babel---compiling\\">Babel - Compiling</h3>\\r\\n<p>Install babel:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">yarn add -D @babel/core @babel/preset-env</code></pre>\\r\\n<p>We next need to tell Babel how to build the code by creating a babel config file:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// babel.config.js</span>\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">module</span>.exports = <span class=\\"hljs-function\\">(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">api</span>) =&gt;</span> {\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> env = api.env();\\r\\n\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// &#x27;test&#x27; requires a node environment (&#x27;test&#x27; is set by jest automatically)</span>\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// &#x27;legacy&#x27; older environments that dont support esmodules</span>\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// &#x27;modern&#x27; (default) is for environments that support esmodules</span>\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> targets = env === <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;test&#x27;</span> ? { <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">node</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;current&#x27;</span> } : env === <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;legacy&#x27;</span> ? <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;&gt;0.25%, not dead&#x27;</span> : { <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">esmodules</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span> };\\r\\n\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> {\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">presets</span>: [\\r\\n            [\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;@babel/preset-env&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n                {\\r\\n                    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">bugfixes</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\r\\n                    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">modules</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">false</span>,\\r\\n                    targets,\\r\\n                },\\r\\n            ],\\r\\n        ],\\r\\n    };\\r\\n};</code></pre>\\r\\n<p>This babel.config file is designed to be run more than once with different environments, to get different outputs. The options are &#39;test&#39;, &#39;legacy&#39; or &#39;modern&#39; (default). </p>\\r\\n<p>We use <code>api.env();</code> to get the value set to &#39;NODE_ENV&#39;, then set the target for Babel to compile too.</p>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><code>test</code> is not important for this example, but common enough to include it for this example. Jest sets <code>NODE_ENV</code> to <code>test</code> automatically and Jest uses Node to run tests, so we set that as the output target.</li>\\r\\n<li><code>legacy</code> is any target that has over a 0.25% market share and not &#39;dead&#39;, that includes IE 11.</li>\\r\\n<li><code>modern</code> is any target that supports ES Modules</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n<p>Other settings:</p>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><code>modules: false</code> is to tell babel not to convert ES Modules to CJS modules, we will use a bundler for that.</li>\\r\\n<li><code>bugfixes: true</code> A new (v7.9.0+) setting for <code>@babel/preset-env</code> that should result in <a href=\\"https://babeljs.io/blog/2020/03/16/7.9.0#babelpreset-envs-bugfixes-option-11083httpsgithubcombabelbabelpull11083\\">smaller compiled code</a> as it fixes with more fine grain than default and it will be the default behaviour in Babel 8.</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n<p>Although over time browsers that support modules will become &#39;legacy&#39; as well, for a while it will mean a much smaller build.</p>\\r\\n<details>\\r\\n<summary>Note About Babel Configuration</summary>\\r\\n<p>Babel can be configured in various ways, in this example we create a function to give us a lot of control.</p>\\r\\n\\r\\n<p>We could have used a browserlist file as well, then have <a href=\\"https://babeljs.io/docs/en/babel-preset-env#browserslist-integration\\">different targets per environment.</a> but doesnt handle the <code>esmodules: true</code> target.</p>\\r\\n\\r\\n<p>Or we could use a babel.config in json format instead that handles different environments differently, however that involves merging of babel files, which I feel is harder to read and requires knowledge of the merging algorithm.</p>\\r\\n</details>\\r\\n\\r\\n<h3 id=\\"rollup\\">Rollup</h3>\\r\\n<p>In this example I have used rollup, but it is possible to use Webpack or even babel directly (see <a href=\\"https://github.com/developit/htm/blob/master/package.json\\">htm&#39;s package.json</a> as good example) and the concepts work the same. We are using it to create different types of bundles in different formats and works with the environments setup in babel config.</p>\\r\\n<p>Install rollup:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">yarn add -D rollup @rollup/plugin-babel rollup-plugin-terser </code></pre>\\r\\n<p>Create a rollup config file:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// rollup.config.js</span>\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> pkg <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;./package.json&#x27;</span>;\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { terser } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;rollup-plugin-terser&#x27;</span>;\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> babel <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;@rollup/plugin-babel&#x27;</span>;\\r\\n\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> banner = <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">`// <span class=\\"hljs-subst\\">${pkg.name}</span> <span class=\\"hljs-subst\\">${pkg.version}</span>`</span>;\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> env = process.env.NODE_ENV;\\r\\n\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">let</span> output;\\r\\n\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">if</span> (env === <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;legacy&#x27;</span>) {\\r\\n    output = {\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">input</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;src/index.js&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">plugins</span>: [babel({ <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">babelHelpers</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;bundled&#x27;</span> })],\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">output</span>: [\\r\\n            {\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">file</span>: pkg.browser,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">format</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;iife&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">name</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;myLibrary&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">plugins</span>: [terser()],\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">sourcemap</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\r\\n                banner,\\r\\n            },\\r\\n            {\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">file</span>: pkg.main,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">format</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;cjs&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">exports</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;named&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">plugins</span>: [terser()],\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">sourcemap</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\r\\n                banner,\\r\\n            },\\r\\n        ],\\r\\n    };\\r\\n}\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// modern</span>\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">else</span> {\\r\\n    output = {\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">input</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;src/index.js&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">plugins</span>: [babel({ <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">babelHelpers</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;bundled&#x27;</span> })],\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">output</span>: [\\r\\n            {\\r\\n                banner,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">file</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;dist/my-library.es.js&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">format</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;es&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">exports</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;named&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">plugins</span>: [terser({ <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">ecma</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">2017</span>, <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">safari10</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span> })],\\r\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">sourcemap</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\r\\n            },\\r\\n        ],\\r\\n    };\\r\\n}\\r\\n\\r\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">export</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">default</span> output;</code></pre>\\r\\n<p>There is a lot going on here. First we create two different build configurations, one we want to generate the legacy builds and another for the <code>modern</code> builds. Which will run is dependent on whether <code>NODE_ENV</code> is <code>legacy</code> or <code>modern</code> when Rollup is called from the command line, it will also effect which babel configuration is used as shown earlier.</p>\\r\\n<p><em><strong>NB:</strong> We didnt create a build for &#39;test&#39; mentioned earlier, as that was just for Jest to use, not for output of the library.</em></p>\\r\\n<p>The <strong>modern</strong> configuration:</p>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><code>input: &#39;src/index.js&#39;</code> The entry point into library</li>\\r\\n<li><code>plugins: [babel({ babelHelpers: &#39;bundled&#39; })]</code> We tell rollup we want to compile the code so it can work in certain environments. <code>&#39;bundled&#39;</code> means that any helper functions babel creates to make sure your code works in that environment are add to your bundled file and not external.</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n<p>There is a single output configuration, that creates a single file:</p>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><code>banner</code> puts the name and version number as a comment at the top of the file (created further up) and taken from the package.json file.</li>\\r\\n<li><code>file: &#39;dist/my-library.es.js&#39;</code> the folder and name of the file to create</li>\\r\\n<li><code>format: &#39;es&#39;</code> tells rollup to generate the bundled with ES Module exports, so it can be used with environments that support it (Node 14+ and newer browsers)</li>\\r\\n<li><code>exports: &#39;named&#39;</code> tells rollup we are not using default exports e.g. <code>export default xxx</code></li>\\r\\n<li><code>sourcemap: true</code> create a souremap file to help with debugging the output as if it was in the original format</li>\\r\\n<li><code>plugins: [terser({ ecma: 2017, safari10: true })]</code> we are telling rollup to minify the code that&#39;s output so its small in size. However we are also telling the minifier which optimisations it can make. By saying its <code>ecma: 2017</code> means it knows it will be able to use certain features, e.g. arrow functions to reduce file size further. <code>safari10</code> means it will handle the bugs in Safari 10/11 with regards to modules.</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n<p>Because the target for environment modern is targeted at <code>{ targets: &#39;esmodules&#39; }</code> the build should be small and lightweight.</p>\\r\\n<p>To run this build we can run:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">NODE_ENV=modern npx rollup -c</code></pre>\\r\\n<p>The <strong>legacy</strong> configuration creates 2 builds:</p>\\r\\n<p>The <code>input</code> and <code>plugins</code> configuration are the same as for the &#39;modern&#39; build above but we have two output configurations this time: </p>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><code>format: &#39;iife&#39;</code> creates a self executing function (IIFE) file for direct use in the browser that can be used in older browsers as well. By giving it a <code>name: &#39;myLibrary&#39;</code> we tell it what global variable to create, without polluting the global namespace with the rest of the code. It can then be accessed throughout <code>myLibrary.doThing();</code></li>\\r\\n<li><code>format: &#39;cjs&#39;</code> creates a CommonJS file that can run in Node.</li>\\r\\n<li><code>plugins: [terser()]</code> we stick with terser&#39;s default settings which will revert to ES5 support.</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n<p>To run the legacy build:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">NODE_ENV=legacy npx rollup -c</code></pre>\\r\\n<p>To run both the builds together:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-bash\\">NODE_ENV=modern npx rollup -c &amp;&amp; NODE_ENV=legacy npx rollup -c</code></pre>\\r\\n<p><em><strong>Tip:</strong> To improve the speed of the build you could run the builds in parallel with an application like <a href=\\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/npm-run-all\\">npm-run-all</a></em></p>\\r\\n<h2 id=\\"entry-points\\">Entry Points</h2>\\r\\n<p>We need to tell environments that import our library where the entry point is:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-json\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// package.json</span>\\r\\n{\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">&quot;main&quot;</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&quot;./dist/myLibrary.cjs.js&quot;</span>,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">&quot;module&quot;</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&quot;./dist/es/index.js&quot;</span>,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">&quot;browser&quot;</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&quot;./dist/myLibrary.min.js&quot;</span>,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">&quot;exports&quot;</span>: {\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">&quot;require&quot;</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&quot;./dist/myLibrary.cjs.js&quot;</span>,\\r\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">&quot;default&quot;</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&quot;./dist/es/index.js&quot;</span>\\r\\n    },\\r\\n}</code></pre>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><p><code>main</code> is the legacy entry that Node (&lt;14) and older bundlers use. Usually in CJS format</p>\\r\\n</li>\\r\\n<li><p><code>module</code> is a &#39;none-standard&#39; but often used field for telling bundlers where the tree-shakeable ES Module version entry point. This is used by default with webpack and rollup.</p>\\r\\n</li>\\r\\n<li><p><code>browser</code> is &#39;none-standard&#39; and even though its used it doesnt really work to tell any specific environment of the entry point. It can be a pointer to say where the bundle that works directly in the bundle is located.</p>\\r\\n</li>\\r\\n<li><p><code>exports</code> is for new Node (14+) and if present makes Node ignore <code>main</code>. There is a lot of <a href=\\"https://nodejs.org/api/packages.html#packages_package_entry_points\\">options</a> with how to use it, but for our library we just need to tell it the entry point. </p>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><p><code>require</code> if the application imports the library using the CommonJS format <code>const myLibrary = require(&#39;my-library&#39;);</code> then use this as the entry point</p>\\r\\n</li>\\r\\n<li><p><code>default</code> if the application uses something other than <code>require</code> then use this, which should be if it is imported using the <code>import</code> keyword <code>import myLibrary from &#39;my-library&#39;;</code> as an ES Module. </p>\\r\\n<p><em><strong>NB:</strong> Could be <code>import</code> rather than <code>default</code>.</em></p>\\r\\n</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n</li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n<h2 id=\\"there-is-a-catch---compiling-modules\\">There is a catch - Compiling Modules...</h2>\\r\\n<p>Everything above should work fine, bundlers will attempt to use the <code>module</code> field by default (currently the <code>exports</code> field is not used by bundlers) which is great as its clean code that&#39;s compiled to be fairly lightweight.</p>\\r\\n<p>But what about applications that install the library but want to use it in a target that is not supported? It might not work in those environments.</p>\\r\\n<p>When setting up a bundler most of us exclude the node_module folders from compilation to speed up the build process, as a slow build can be annoying. But that means every piece of code you install and bundle has to work for your weakest environment, and in fact it could be worse as the library author might have picked a really old environment as its build target, so it is not ideal as you would be importing slower and larger code than you likely need.</p>\\r\\n<p>I compile the ES Modules bundle so that it is lightweight with few changes, that means if you want your application to run against older software you should make sure you include either all of node_modules, or make sure you dont exclude </p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\">exclude: <span class=\\"hljs-regexp\\">/[\\\\\\\\/]node_modules[\\\\\\\\/](?!fn-validate)/</span>,</code></pre>\\r\\n<p>You would really need to inform your users of this in your documentation.</p>\\r\\n<p><em><strong>Opinion:</strong> I personally believe a none-compiled (or lightly compiled) version of code should be shipped with most libraries to enable smaller code and lighter weight bundles.</em></p>\\r\\n<h3 id=\\"modulesnomodules\\">Modules/nomodules</h3>\\r\\n<p>This bundle set up, also works for to the modules/nomodules pattern, where you can import the newer code is modules are supported, or fallback to the legacy version. As discussed by <a href=\\"https://jakearchibald.com/2017/es-modules-in-browsers\\">Jake Archibald</a>.</p>\\r\\n<p>Although over time, browsers that support modules will start to fall behind the current batch and we will need to come up with a new way of delivering our legacy and modern code :)</p>\\r\\n<h2 id=\\"taking-it-further---splitting-es-module-files\\">Taking it Further - Splitting ES Module files</h2>\\r\\n<p>The above is all you need to do to build the environments described however with HTTP/2 being prevalent, you might want to consider building and exporting each module individually:</p>\\r\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// rollup.config.js</span>\\r\\n{\\r\\n    banner,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">dir</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;dist/es&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">format</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;es&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">exports</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;named&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">preserveModules</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">preserveModulesRoot</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">&#x27;src&#x27;</span>,\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">plugins</span>: [terser({ <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">ecma</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">2017</span>, <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">safari10</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span> })],\\r\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">sourcemap</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\r\\n},</code></pre>\\r\\n<p>By adding the output config to the modern build of our <code>rollup.config.js</code> we can also create a full file structure based on our src files. <code>preserveModules: true</code> says rather than bundle the files together to compile and output them per module, <code>preserveModulesRoot: &#39;src&#39;</code> tells rollup the <code>src</code> folder is the root for the output.</p>\\r\\n<p>Then each file can be imported as desired directly.</p>\\r\\n<h2 id=\\"thoughts\\">Thoughts</h2>\\r\\n<p>We are now at a point where CommonJS requirement is starting to die, but its not completely gone yet. Soon however we should be able to think about just supporting legacy browsers and also ES modules for both Node and the browser.</p>\\r\\n<h2 id=\\"references\\">References</h2>\\r\\n<ul>\\r\\n<li><a href=\\"https://philipwalton.com/articles/deploying-es2015-code-in-production-today/\\">https://philipwalton.com/articles/deploying-es2015-code-in-production-today/</a></li>\\r\\n<li><a href=\\"https://camillehdl.dev/ship-modern-javascript-rollup/\\">https://camillehdl.dev/ship-modern-javascript-rollup/</a></li>\\r\\n<li><a href=\\"https://nodejs.medium.com/announcing-core-node-js-support-for-ecmascript-modules-c5d6dc29b663\\">https://nodejs.medium.com/announcing-core-node-js-support-for-ecmascript-modules-c5d6dc29b663</a></li>\\r\\n<li><a href=\\"https://nodejs.org/api/packages.html\\">https://nodejs.org/api/packages.html</a></li>\\r\\n</ul>\\r\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["treeshaking","webpack","babel","javascript","rollup"]}')}}]);